GitOps with Docker + Kubernetes Deployment

✨ Hello DevOps Learners!
Continuing my GitOps learning journey after completing Ansible, today I’m connecting Docker + Kubernetes with GitOps to build a complete end-to-end DevOps workflow 🚀
This is how modern applications are deployed in real production environments.

🔹 Container Deployment using Docker
 Docker is used to package applications into containers.
Application + dependencies bundled together
Runs consistently across environments
Easy to deploy and scale
Once built, the Docker image is pushed to a container registry.

🔹 Kubernetes Deployment
 Kubernetes is used to:
Deploy containers
Manage scaling and availability
Handle networking and load balancing
Deployment is defined using YAML files (Deployment + Service).

🔹 GitOps Integration
 Here’s the complete GitOps workflow:
1️⃣ Developer writes code
 2️⃣ CI tool builds Docker image
 3️⃣ Image pushed to registry
 4️⃣ Deployment YAML updated in Git
 5️⃣ GitOps tool detects change
 6️⃣ Kubernetes cluster gets updated automatically

🔹 End-to-End DevOps Flow
 Code → Build → Docker → Git → GitOps → Kubernetes
Everything is automated and controlled through Git.

🔹 Why This Matters
Faster deployments
Consistent environments
Easy rollback using Git
Fully automated pipeline

💡 Key Insight:
 GitOps combined with Docker and Kubernetes creates a powerful, automated, and production-ready deployment system.
